UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 1: Choosing a research topic', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort: (1) dinosaurs (2) How do kangaroo joeys grow in the pouch? Which is a better focused topic and why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFocused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.\n\nCHECK\nA topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nList five interesting topics, narrow each to a how/why question, rate sources you can actually find and choose one research question for the unit.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: List five interesting topics, narrow each to a how/why question, rate sources you can actually find and choose one research question for the unit. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing. Reasoning: I used topic by attending to interest and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Correction: Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing. Why: the correction restores accurate use of topic.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating topic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with topic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing. This matches the lesson skill for topic.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Sort: (1) dinosaurs (2) How do kangaroo joeys grow in the pouch? Which is a better focused topic and why?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources.","Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","Careful use of interest and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named interest and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the interest and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising topic. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries topic.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show topic and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses topic from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise interest and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about topic."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of topic to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating topic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing. Reasoning: I used topic by attending to interest and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with topic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Correction: Focused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing. Why: the correction restores accurate use of topic.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w36-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise topic.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use topic with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify interest and explain why it matters.","I can practise focus independently on a new prompt.","I can notice researchable needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort: (1) dinosaurs (2) How do kangaroo joeys grow in the pouch? Which is a better focused topic and why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFocused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","explain":"Choosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Focused research question vs a huge topic.","caption":"Interest must meet a manageable focus.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Focused</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How honeybees find flowers</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Too broad</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everything about insects</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Source availability as a topic test.","caption":"Choose questions you can actually investigate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Researchable</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">kid-friendly sources exist</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stall risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no sources available</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Here is a more productive model: \"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFocused question (2) is better for a short report; (1) is too broad without narrowing.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A topic with no available kid-friendly sources will stall the project. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChoosing a research topic means picking something interesting, narrow enough to manage and possible to research with available sources. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 1: Choosing a research topic","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Research project and portfolio","week":36,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"How honeybees find flowers\" is more researchable for Year 3 than \"everything about insects\". Interest + focus + sources.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Focused research question vs a huge topic.","caption":"Interest must meet a manageable focus.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"3